  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes the main physical barriers that a delivery system faces when trying to move through a plant and into specific organelles, and pairs each barrier with general design strategies to overcome it. A whole-plant schematic links to enlarged cross-sections of leaves, stems, roots, and cells, showing that access is restricted at several nested levels.\n\nAt the tissue scale, the cuticle and epidermis form a hydrophobic outer barrier, while stomata appear as limited entry points. Inside the stem, the vascular bundle is divided into phloem and xylem, emphasizing that transport pathways differ by size and charge constraints. In roots, the epidermis and especially the Casparian strip appear to regulate passage toward the vascular tissue.\n\nAt the cellular scale, the cell wall, plasma membrane, and vacuole create another major bottleneck before cargos can reach the cytosol. The figure then zooms in further to chloroplast and mitochondrial membranes, each shown as double membranes with transport channels that tightly control entry.\n\nThe lower table condenses these ideas: each barrier presents a distinct delivery challenge, and bypass approaches include mechanical or chemical assistance, tuning carrier size and charge, exploiting vascular transport behavior, and using biomimetic targeting motifs for plastids and mitochondria.",
